# The `squash-layers` tool (design/stacks.md, "Publishing"): the std/go worker
# image with this directory's program curried onto it, plus the `help` it is
# described by. A writer run on the conversation: `@in` hands it the
# conversation tree, and it proposes that tree with `into` replaced.
HELP=<<END
Squash each layer of a stack into one commit, for publishing each layer as a
branch with a linear history, and write them to `into` as one folder of
gitlinks, {<entry>: <commit>}, replacing what a previous run wrote there. The
layers keep their own merge history; this only mints new commits beside them.

The layers are the files in `messages`, in byte order: one per layer to publish,
named after its entry in `stack`, holding that layer's full commit message
(subject, blank line, body), used verbatim. An entry with no message file, such
as 00-base, is not published.

For each layer in order, C = commit(tree = the layer tip's tree, parent = the
previous C, or `onto` for the first layer, message = that layer's file), with the
author and committer copied from the layer tip. The same input always mints the
same commits. Refused, naming both, unless each layer's tip already contains the
one below it (or `onto`, for the first): merge a changed lower layer up first.

Then publish_source each <into>/<entry>, bottom to top, with rewrite=true.
@param {tree} stack Conversation path of the folder holding the layer gitlinks, e.g. `feature`.
@param {commit} onto Full hash of the commit the first layer is published onto: the base branch's tip that layer 1 last merged.
@param {tree} messages Conversation path of a folder holding one commit-message file per layer to publish, named after its stack entry, e.g. `feature-messages` holding `01-core` and `02-tests`.
@param into Conversation path to write the squashed stack to, e.g. `publish/feature`: a new path, or one a previous run wrote. Not inside a source tree.
@writer
@in
END
curry --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/go --worker1:@=worker.go --help=$HELP
